WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.480
The stars of "Jurassic Park" have been in&nbsp;
some certified classics – and they've also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:09.440
been in some of the worst movies ever made.&nbsp;
From straight-to-video sequels to films that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.640
zero critics liked, these are the horrible movies&nbsp;
"Jurassic Park" actors hope you forget about.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:20.320
You'd be hard-pressed to find a more reputable&nbsp;
actress in Hollywood today than Laura Dern.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:25.840
She has starred in more than 60 feature films,&nbsp;
and was even nominated for two Academy Awards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:31.760
before finally taking home her first Oscar in 2020&nbsp;
for her poignant performance in "Marriage Story."

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:37.840
Dern has been appearing in movies since 1973,&nbsp;
and throughout her decades-spanning career she's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:42.640
shone in some truly unforgettable roles,&nbsp;
arguably none more popular than that of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:48.960
paleobotanist Ellie Sattler in "Jurassic Park."&nbsp;
If Dr. Sattler is Dern's most memorable role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.840
however, perhaps her most forgettable&nbsp;
part came years later in 2010's undeniably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.800
lackluster "Little Fockers," the third and final&nbsp;
installment in the "Meet the Parents" trilogy.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:04.080
Despite reuniting Ben Stiller, Robert De Niro,&nbsp;
and Dustin Hoffman, the trilogy-capper ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:09.040
the once-promising comedy series on a very&nbsp;
low note. Most people probably don't even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.680
remember that this movie exists, let alone&nbsp;
that Laura Dern plays a very small part in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:19.120
it as an elementary school headmistress named&nbsp;
Prudence. Ironically enough, Dern must not have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:24.080
been exercising much prudence when she decided&nbsp;
to sign on for this terribly unfunny comedy.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:30.160
Before he got ripped to play Star-Lord in&nbsp;
2014's "Guardians of the Galaxy," Chris Pratt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:34.800
wasn't exactly the guy that movie studios had&nbsp;
on speed dial when they needed an action hero.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:35.520
"Starlord."

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:37.780
"Who?"

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:40.880
"Starlord, man. The legendary outlaw?"

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:45.600
Fast-forward a few years, however,&nbsp;
and things have certainly changed.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.160
In addition to his wildly popular portrayal&nbsp;
of the Galaxy's favorite snarky hero in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.520
"Guardians of the Galaxy" movies,&nbsp;
Pratt has also headlined another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:59.120
multimillion-dollar franchise as velociraptor&nbsp;
trainer Owen Grady in 2015's "Jurassic World."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:03.280
As Grady, Pratt got to showcase his&nbsp;
effortless acting chops with both bold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:07.120
unapologetic machismo and&nbsp;
grounded everyman humility.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:13.200
He's also been a part of a movie that failed on&nbsp;
almost every level. "Movie 43," which was marketed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:19.200
as a star-studded racy comedy, is undoubtedly&nbsp;
the worst film on Pratt's resume. With a lowly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:25.680
4% score on Rotten Tomatoes, this jumbled mess&nbsp;
of a movie took home three Razzies in 2014,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:30.240
including the Worst Picture award. While Pratt&nbsp;
shouldn't shoulder much of the blame for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:35.840
awful final product, you have to assume that he&nbsp;
hopes "Movie 43" never comes up in interviews.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:40.480
Jeff Goldblum is the kind of performer who&nbsp;
can take virtually any role and make it a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:46.000
fan favorite. His filmography consists of&nbsp;
over 135 acting credits and is chock full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:51.440
of unforgettable movies and characters, including&nbsp;
Seth Brundle in 1986's "The Fly," David Levinson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:56.800
in 1996's "Independence Day," and of course,&nbsp;
Dr. Ian Malcolm in 1993's "Jurassic Park."

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:02.000
Goldblum's unique acting prowess is beyond&nbsp;
question, but with such an expansive filmography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:07.120
there are bound to be a few misses to coincide&nbsp;
with all the hits. Perhaps his most notable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:12.640
failure was that of 2015's "Mortdecai," in&nbsp;
which Goldblum plays a shady art dealer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:18.000
Milton Krampf. Arguably the worst Johnny Depp&nbsp;
movie of all time, "Mortdecai" plays like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:23.520
cheap "Austin Powers" knockoff. Even the talented&nbsp;
core cast consisting of Depp, Gwyneth Paltrow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:28.560
and Ewan McGregor couldn't save this action&nbsp;
comedy from colossally flopping at the box office.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:33.040
Vincent D'Onofrio has a knack&nbsp;
for playing really good bad guys.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:37.440
You might not have even recognized him&nbsp;
as Edgar, the creepy unwitting host of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:43.360
nefarious cockroach alien in 1997's "Men in&nbsp;
Black," or as the bald crime boss, Kingpin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:48.240
in Marvel's "Daredevil" and "Hawkeye." He&nbsp;
also played the main antagonist Hoskins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:52.960
the head of InGen's security force, in "Jurassic&nbsp;
World." A self-proclaimed method actor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:57.520
D'Onofrio has turned in some incredibly&nbsp;
believable performances over the years.

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:01.200
However, there have been a few less&nbsp;
than believable performances, too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:06.640
In 2017's "Rings," a sequel to 2002's "The&nbsp;
Ring," D'Onofrio played a blind caretaker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:12.400
with a secret connection to the ghostly Samara.&nbsp;
As Peter Sobczynski of RogerEbert.com put it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.800
he seems to be playing the role for an&nbsp;
easy paycheck and absolutely nothing else.

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:20.960
Not exactly the kind of criticism you'd&nbsp;
expect to hear about an actor who once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:25.760
gained a whopping 70 pounds just to play&nbsp;
Private Pyle in "Full Metal Jacket."

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:30.960
"This is my rifle! There are many&nbsp;
like it, but this one is mine!"

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:35.200
Still, D'Onofrio was hardly the main&nbsp;
issue in "Rings," an incoherent film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:38.080
that's branded with an 8%&nbsp;
rating on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:44.080
Sam Neill is no stranger to being in front of&nbsp;
the camera. With nearly 150 acting credits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:49.120
the New Zealand native has been performing in&nbsp;
movies and TV shows since the early '70s. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:54.480
most recognizable role is obviously that of Dr.&nbsp;
Alan Grant from the "Jurassic Park" franchise, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:59.600
he's also starred in plenty of excellent films.&nbsp;
"Irresistible," however, is not one of them.

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:04.960
Despite a talented leading trio of Neill, Susan&nbsp;
Sarandon, and a young, pre-fame Emily Blunt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:10.480
this 2006 dramatic thriller went straight to DVD.&nbsp;
The premise was simple enough: Sarandon becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:15.840
suspicious that she's being stalked by Neill's new&nbsp;
secretary, played by Blunt, and paranoia ensues.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:20.800
It's honestly quite similar to other popular&nbsp;
movies like "The Hand That Rocks the Cradle"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:25.520
and "Sleeping with the Enemy," but lacks any of&nbsp;
those films' ability to tell a compelling story.

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:31.520
"Irresistible" currently boasts a 20% critical&nbsp;
score on Rotten Tomatoes, which could certainly be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:37.200
worse. However, what's arguably the grossest facet&nbsp;
of the movie is the uncomfortable age gap between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:44.640
Sam Neill and Emily Blunt. Neill was born in 1947,&nbsp;
and Blunt was born in 1983, making him roughly 36&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:49.360
years older than his co-star. It's quite an&nbsp;
age gap, even for a Hollywood blockbuster.

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:54.480
During a 2020 appearance on The Graham&nbsp;
Norton Show, legendary actor Samuel L.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:57.440
Jackson proudly declared that he&nbsp;
loves watching his own movies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:02.800
Apparently, not only does he buy $1,000 worth&nbsp;
of tickets for his new films when they first hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:07.520
theaters, but he also frequently rewatches his&nbsp;
older flicks when he's bored at home. He said,

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:09.600
"If I'm channel surfing and&nbsp;
there's nothing else on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:14.320
I'll go into the search engine and go 'Samuel&nbsp;
L. Jackson...' I pick one and I watch it."

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:17.680
While Jackson has been in too&nbsp;
many iconic movies to even name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:21.520
there's one film, in particular, that's hard&nbsp;
to imagine he would ever want to rewatch.

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:23.120
"Hold onto your butts."

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:29.280
In 2004, Oscar-nominated screenwriter Philip&nbsp;
Kaufman directed a movie called "Twisted,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:34.720
a mystery thriller that stars Ashley Judd as a&nbsp;
police detective trying to catch a serial killer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:38.880
Jackson played the police commissioner who,&nbsp;
unsurprisingly is revealed to be the killer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:43.440
Despite the combined star power of Judd,&nbsp;
Jackson, and Andy Garcia, critics found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:48.720
incredibly little to like about "Twisted,"&nbsp;
blasting it down to a 1% on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:54.240
Ariana Richards was only 12 years old when&nbsp;
she played Lex Murphy, the adolescent computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:59.520
nerd hacker in "Jurassic Park," but she was&nbsp;
apparently so good at displaying genuine terror,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:03.200
that even director Steven Spielberg&nbsp;
was impressed. As she told Den of Geek,

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:09.120
"When we were filming the scene of the T-rex with&nbsp;
the jeep, … Steven came over to me once and said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:13.920
'Ariana, you reach such a deep level of&nbsp;
fear and terror –- what do you draw from?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:18.560
Were you scared by a clown when you were&nbsp;
three? Don't tell me, I don't want to know!'"

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:22.720
Perhaps one of the events that Richards drew&nbsp;
fear from was reading critics' reviews of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:26.800
"Spaced Invaders," a movie she starred&nbsp;
in three years before "Jurassic Park."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:30.320
The hammy sci-fi comedy followed a&nbsp;
group of short, bumbling aliens who,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:33.600
after overhearing a radio broadcast&nbsp;
of "War of the Worlds," invade Earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:38.720
on Halloween night, only to be mistaken for&nbsp;
trick-or-treaters in a small American town.

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:42.480
Although audiences didn't seem to hate&nbsp;
"Spaced Invaders," critics tore it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:46.160
pieces in their reviews. Roger&nbsp;
Ebert bashed the film, writing,

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:48.960
"One of the purposes of growing&nbsp;
up and getting an education is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:52.160
to learn why movies like 'Spaced&nbsp;
Invaders' are a waste of time."

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:57.360
Of all the actors in "Jurassic Park,"&nbsp;
only BD Wong returned to reprise his role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:01.760
that of InGen's chief geneticist&nbsp;
Dr. Henry Wu, in "Jurassic World."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:05.360
Although he was reportedly hesitant&nbsp;
to slip back into Dr. Wu's shoes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:09.840
Wong once again found himself right smack&nbsp;
dab in the middle of a dino disaster.

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:15.680
The "Jurassic Park" franchise is undoubtedly&nbsp;
Wong's most popular work, but he's also excelled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:20.320
in other areas throughout the years, including&nbsp;
successful TV series like "Gotham," "Mr. Robot,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:25.360
and "Law &amp; Order: Special Victims Unit." He&nbsp;
also voiced Shang in Disney's animated classic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:30.960
"Mulan." However, it hasn't all been sunshine&nbsp;
and critical roses for the former Broadway star.

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:35.200
In 1998, Wong had the misfortune&nbsp;
of starring in "The Substitute 2:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:40.080
School's Out," a straight-to-cable sequel&nbsp;
with almost no connection to its 1996 crime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:43.520
thriller predecessor. Wong played&nbsp;
one of the bad guys, but honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:48.640
the lazy outing is so poorly put together that&nbsp;
the good guys are pretty bad, too. The movie has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:54.640
a rare 0% Rotten Tomatoes score — not exactly the&nbsp;
kind of grade you want on your acting report card.

00:08:55.680 --> 00:09:01.040
When "The 5th Wave" hit theaters in January of&nbsp;
2016, it's easy to imagine that Sony and Columbia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:05.760
Pictures were banking on a possible franchise.&nbsp;
After all, the movie was based on the first novel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:10.560
in Rick Yancey's literary trilogy, so there's&nbsp;
definitely plenty of source material to work with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:15.680
However, after an onslaught of dismal reviews&nbsp;
and a very disappointing box office pull,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:19.040
any future sequels seem to&nbsp;
be unlikely at this point.

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:22.000
"Are we still alive?"
"I think so."

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:27.600
In the explosive sci-fi flick, Nick Robinson, who&nbsp;
co-starred as an angsty teen amidst a dinosaur&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:33.120
breakout in "Jurassic World," plays an angsty&nbsp;
teen amidst an alien invasion. Co-starring with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:38.640
Chloë Grace Moretz, Ron Livingston, and Liev&nbsp;
Schreiber, the "5th Wave" cast tried their best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:43.360
to keep the ambitious action movie from imploding&nbsp;
into a million pieces, but they were ultimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:48.880
unsuccessful. While it's hardly the first young&nbsp;
adult novel movie adaptation to flop, you have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:53.760
assume that Robinson wishes he would've just said&nbsp;
"pass" when the casting director came calling.

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:58.640
Have you ever watched a movie that's so&nbsp;
over-the-top emotional that it seems as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:02.560
if the whole film was created with the&nbsp;
sole purpose of making the audience cry?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:07.920
That's exactly what it feels like to watch 1995's&nbsp;
"Three Wishes," a sappy family drama starring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:13.520
Patrick Swayze, Mary Elizabeth Mastrantonio,&nbsp;
and "Jurassic Park" child star Joseph Mazzello.

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:17.200
The story follows a mother&nbsp;
who lets a drifter named Jack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:20.720
stay with her and her two sons after&nbsp;
accidentally hitting him with her car.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:25.440
As you can probably predict, there's more to&nbsp;
Jack than meets the eye, and he begins to form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:29.280
a special bond with the young boys, becoming&nbsp;
the father figure that they were missing.

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:34.320
It's easy to criticize corny movies like this,&nbsp;
but people do find enjoyment in clichéd feel-good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:38.800
stories. This probably helps explain the&nbsp;
52% audience score that "Three Wishes"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:43.680
holds on Rotten Tomatoes. Critics, however,&nbsp;
had different opinions. Roger Ebert wrote,

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:47.520
"The movie lays it on so thick that&nbsp;
even with the best will in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:50.480
I couldn't go along on its slow and soppy ride."

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:57.120
For whatever reason, film adaptations of Dan&nbsp;
Brown's crowd-pleasing Robert Langdon novels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.120 --> 00:11:01.200
like "The Da Vinci Code" and "Angels and&nbsp;
Demons," are never well received by critics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:05.680
Even with Ron Howard in the director's&nbsp;
chair and Tom Hanks playing the lead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:08.800
the mystery thrillers just don't&nbsp;
translate to the big screen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:14.240
Hanks' third attempt at Langdon, 2016's&nbsp;
"Inferno," was another example of this.

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:18.960
"Inferno" starred Hanks and "Rogue One" breakout&nbsp;
Felicity Jones as the two main characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:24.160
but it also had a solid supporting cast, including&nbsp;
two guys who had just starred in "Jurassic World"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:30.960
the year before, Omar Sy and the late Irrfan&nbsp;
Khan. Although both actors are extremely talented,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:35.120
Sy was nominated for a Golden Globe&nbsp;
for his work on Netflix's "Lupin," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:39.360
Khan accrued a slew of accolades for his&nbsp;
work in both Hollywood and India, not even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:43.760
their acting abilities could save "Inferno"&nbsp;
from going up in flames at the box office.

